Observing the Course as a Pedagogical Partner
For the fall 2026 semester, I’m working with Professor Shiro Kuriwaki as a pedagogical partner for Applied Quantitative Research Design through Yale’s Poorvu Center for Teaching and Learning. My role is to help the teaching team understand how students experience the course. I observe classes, take notes on teaching and student engagement, and discuss my observations with the teaching team. I also collect student feedback to understand what supports or hinders their learning. In this blog, I will share reflections from our class as we grappled with how AI tools are reshaping student learning and how effective cold calling is for student participation and guaging student understanding. I hope these reflections offer other instructors useful questions to ask about their own courses.

AI Tools in the Class
One question we’re working through this semester is how AI use affects student learning. Instead of prohibiting AI, we hope this course prepares students with the skills for research and the workplace by learning to be savvy and discerning with AI. Thus, the course encourages students to use AI for coding. At the same time we want them to learn to explain the code and understand the concepts underlying the coding. The course pairs AI use with closed-book exams and an oral exam to assess students’ understanding. 
Shiro implemented a custom AI bot on Ed Discussion to help answer students’ questions. The teaching team tested the bot on practice questions. While it was able to answer them successfully, Shiro pointed out that some answers were too long and used more notation than students would need for the exam. Still, the course is offering the Ed Discussion bot as a resource for students. Despite having access to the bot, students do not appear to be using it.
I understand the hesitation – in fact the choice by students to not use it makes a lot of sense. Since the course allows other AI tools and students were encouraged to get a coding agent subscription at the beginning of the year, students likely choose to use their own AI tools. For me, the ease and familiarity of my own chatbot outweigh the reasons to try the class bot, especially when its benefits aren’t clear. When I attempted the first pset, I chose the AI I am most familiar with because it’s hard for the Ed Discussion bot compete with my routine using my own AI chatbots. While I can’t speak for all students, this is my intuition on what some may be thinking.
If we continue offering the Ed Discussion bot, I think we need to show students when and why it would be useful (and, why it is preferable than accessing their own AI). A short demonstration using an actual assignment question could show them how to test it out.

Possible revisions for future iterations of the course:
I think it feels inevitable that many students are using AI tools to assist with their work, if not completing the entire assignment. One way for the assignment to adapt to this is requiring a documentation component. The students could submit their chatbot history; this would allow the teaching team to see which parts of the pset the students would consult AI, and what types of prompts or questions students have. (Though, I can’t imagine the teaching team having enough time to go through it manually but using AI to summarize what happened in the chat session could streamline this). This way, AI is still allowed to be used, but rather than uploading the whole assignment for an AI to complete, the students have to break the assignment into parts and go through the effort of showing they are engaging with the AI to get their answer. A less demanding approach is to do a reflection at the end of the assignment, asking students to choose one exchange, include the relevant prompt and response, and explain what they used, changed, or rejected and how they checked the result.
